shortAnswer: "Fine-tuning means taking an existing AI model and training it further on your own examples, so it adopts a particular style or format by default. It changes how the model behaves — not what it knows. Most business problems people reach for fine-tuning to solve are actually prompt problems or data-access problems, and those are far cheaper to fix."
faq:
  - q: "Will fine-tuning teach the model about our business?"
    a: "Not reliably, and this is the most common misunderstanding. Fine-tuning shapes <em>behaviour</em> — tone, format, structure. For facts about your business, you want the model reading your actual documents, which is a retrieval problem, not a training one."
  - q: "How much does it cost?"
    a: "The training run itself is often modest. The real cost is preparing hundreds or thousands of high-quality examples, and then redoing that work each time you want to change something or move to a newer model. That maintenance burden is what catches people out."
  - q: "When is fine-tuning actually the right answer?"
    a: "When you need a consistent, narrow output format at high volume and prompting has genuinely failed — classifying thousands of records into your own categories, say. It's a specialist tool for a specialist job, not a general upgrade."
  - q: "What should we try first?"
    a: "In order: a better prompt, then a system prompt that sets standing rules, then giving the model access to your real documents. That sequence resolves the overwhelming majority of cases, and each step costs a fraction of fine-tuning."
  - q: "Does fine-tuning make the model smarter?"
    a: "No. A fine-tuned smaller model can beat a general larger one at one narrow task, but it doesn't gain general capability — and it often gets worse at everything outside the task you tuned it for."
